1. Definitions and scope

"Licensor" means the CCS maintainer operating under the name Shibxtshi,
established in France, solely to the extent entitled to grant the rights
described here. This description does not establish exclusive ownership
of every part of the repository. Contributors retain their own rights.

"Covered Material" means the original CCS code, documentation,
translations and templates made available under this license, together
with adaptations or modifications of their protectable portions. Code
includes source and executable forms. The official visual identity
accompanying CCS copies is addressed specifically in section 10.

"Third-Party Material" means components subject to separate rights and
licenses, including the Inter and Barlow Condensed font files and any
third-party dependencies. Its inclusion or reference in a package does
not put that material under the CCS license.

"Corresponding Source" means the complete preferred editable form of
the covered version actually provided, including its modifications and
the instructions, manifests and original build scripts needed to
reconstruct that version. General-purpose tools and separate third-party
components remain subject to their own licenses; identify the relevant
versions and means of obtaining them without purporting to relicense
them under CCS. Providing only an unmodified upstream version is not
providing the source of a modified version.

"Internal Scope" includes a person using CCS privately, or an entity,
the companies in its corporate group, and authorized service providers
acting on its behalf and under its control for that internal use.
External customers are not internal users merely because they have an
account, a contract or access to a dedicated instance. A provider may
act within a customer's Internal Scope for operations, but that does
not make the customer part of the provider's own Internal Scope.

"Redistribution" means supplying a copy of Covered Material outside
the applicable Internal Scope. Supplying a commissioned result to an
external client remains subject to sections 4 and 6. Interactive network
use without delivery of a copy is addressed separately in section 8.

"Public Modified Fork" means a modified version of CCS presented or
made available to the public as a separate project or product. Mere
compatibility or a genuinely independent configuration does not, by
itself, make a work a fork or an adaptation of Covered Material.

2. Copyright permissions and independent works

Licensor grants, for the duration of the applicable copyright rights
and subject to these conditions and section 12, a worldwide,
non-exclusive, royalty-free permission to use,
run, study, reproduce and modify Covered Material for personal,
educational, nonprofit, internal and professional purposes, and to
redistribute it under section 4. No ownership is transferred.

This license does not extend to independently created works merely
because they describe, interoperate with or are compatible with CCS.
Independent tutorials, themes, plugins or other creations are not
automatically subject to CCS restrictions. Any actual reproduction or
adaptation of protectable Covered Material must be assessed on its
content. No monopoly over ideas, functionality or interfaces is asserted
merely by this license, and mandatory exceptions remain unaffected.

Your private profiles, credentials and game data are not transferred
to Licensor or made subject to disclosure by these permissions. The
license does not certify originality, non-infringement or exclusivity.

3. Private, internal and professional use

Making or using modifications solely within the Internal Scope does
not require publication or disclosure of those modifications to the
public. Connecting private devices or using an internal network does
not by itself remove this exception. External distribution and external
users of a modified hosted version remain subject to sections 4 and 8.

Earning income from activities using CCS is not, by itself, selling CCS.
Monetized streaming, sponsorship, paid coaching, training and other
professional use are permitted. This permission is distinct from a
charge for copies, software licenses or CCS functional access. Neither
this license nor use of GSI establishes approval by a game publisher,
competitive platform, tournament or anti-cheat provider.

4. Free redistribution, attribution and source delivery

You may redistribute unmodified or modified Covered Material without
charging for the software copy or its license, subject to the genuine
service and commissioned-work provisions in sections 6 and 7. You must:

  (a) retain applicable copyright, license and third-party notices;
  (b) supply the applicable effective CCS license with covered copies;
  (c) identify material changes and not misrepresent a modified version
      as official or supported by Licensor;
  (d) give each recipient of an executable version its complete
      Corresponding Source immediately, free of charge, together with
      the executable delivery; and
  (e) preserve these CCS no-resale and source conditions for covered
      portions and their modifications, without imposing contradictory
      restrictions on the rights granted to recipients.

An additional source link is optional; it does not substitute for
delivery of the complete Corresponding Source to the recipient. A
complete source-only package already supplies its covered source.
These duties apply to the version actually delivered, not an unrelated
or later version. They do not require publishing source to the whole
world when delivery is only to identified recipients.

Keep secrets, personal profiles, PINs, tokens, private operational data,
private signing keys and game data out of source deliveries and source
offers. Supply safe examples and non-secret reconstruction instructions.
This exclusion must not be used to withhold covered source by merely
labeling source code confidential. User data and access to operational
credentials are governed separately by their applicable rights and any
lawful service arrangements, not by a requirement to publish secrets.

5. Prohibited software sales and voluntary donations

You may not sell or require payment for a covered software copy, its
license or continued exercise of its licensed functionality. Prohibited
arrangements include paid software downloads, paid "Pro" editions,
serial resale of covered software or modified editions, mandatory
software subscriptions and charges for access to CCS functionality.
The functional-access restriction applies equally to original and
modified hosted CCS. A nominal price of zero on an invoice does not
make a disguised software sale compliant.

The specifically permitted services in sections 6 and 7 are not
prohibited merely because their genuine labor, infrastructure or
operations are paid for. They must not disguise an otherwise prohibited
copy sale or functional-access subscription.

Entirely voluntary donations and symbolic acknowledgments are allowed.
No donation may be required for a version, feature, early access or
other software entitlement. Donor-only versions, paid early releases
and exclusive CCS functions are not permitted. Optional paid support
must comply independently with section 6; it is not a donor-only
software benefit.

A competent rights holder may give a separate written permission for
acts otherwise restricted here, but only for rights that holder is
authorized to license. No authority over Third-Party Material or other
contributors' ungranted rights is presumed.

6. Commissioned work, independent bundles and optional services

Genuinely commissioned, custom development may be paid for and its
results delivered to the commissioning client. The fee remunerates
the work, not the sale of a CCS copy or a continuing software license.
Attribution and Corresponding Source obligations apply to the covered
result delivered to that client. This exception does not permit serial
resale, standardized paid "Pro" editions, or a nominal commission used
to disguise a software paywall.

Payment for commissioned work does not require disclosure to the world
of otherwise private work. It does not remove the client's license and
source rights in the covered result received. Independent client data
and third-party material remain separate. A contract must not cancel
the CCS redistribution rights actually granted for covered portions.

Independent hardware or services may be sold with CCS supplied free
of charge, provided the covered software is also obtainable separately
without mandatory payment for that hardware or service. This does not
require a developer to perform genuinely commissioned work without pay;
the commissioned-delivery rule above governs that distinct situation.

Optional installation, configuration, support and maintenance services
may be charged for independently. Their fees must concern the services,
not a paid feature, copy or software entitlement. No support or
maintenance subscription is required to keep exercising CCS rights
already obtained, and ending such a service must not revoke those
rights or introduce a software lock. Paid updates or functions cannot
be relabeled "maintenance" to evade section 5. Genuinely commissioned
patches remain governed by the commissioned-work rule above.

7. Paid managed private instances

A managed private CCS instance may be provided for payment when the
remuneration genuinely concerns infrastructure and operations, rather
than selling a CCS license, feature tier or subscription to CCS
functionality. This exception applies to original and modified CCS.

The customer must receive the copies, source and reconstruction
materials to which this license entitles it and be able to run CCS
independently of the provider without buying a CCS software license
or functional-access entitlement. The distinction between service
charges and the free software must be made clear. Merely calling a
functional-access charge "hosting" is insufficient.

A provider may stop supplying unpaid or ended infrastructure and
operations in accordance with its lawful service terms; this license
does not require free infrastructure indefinitely. Such cessation must
not revoke the customer's software rights or justify withholding source
or other materials already due under this license. Any rights concerning
the customer's private data and necessary operational handover remain
subject to applicable law and lawful service arrangements, without
requiring publication of credentials or private data.

Where the hosted version is modified, external users also have the
source access described in section 8, regardless of who pays for the
infrastructure. Customers are not deemed internal to the provider simply
to avoid that obligation.

For the provider-customer relationship, the customer is an external
user of the provider's modified hosted offering and must receive that
source access. Performing authorized technical work within the
customer's Internal Scope does not exempt the provider from this
obligation to its customer.

This copyright/service permission is not a technical validation of
Internet hosting, security, privacy compliance or multi-user isolation.
It does not override the applicable terms of infrastructure providers.

8. Source access for modified hosted versions

If you operate a modified covered version for interactive network use
by users outside the applicable Internal Scope, those external users
must be able to obtain its complete Corresponding Source free of
charge throughout operation of the service. Make this source access
clear and usable without a software payment, donation or support
subscription. Do not provide only the unmodified upstream source.

The source must correspond to the modified version being provided.
Sections 1 and 4 define its scope and the exclusion of secrets and
private data. The requirement does not compel publication of purely
internal modifications to people outside that Internal Scope.

The hosted source trigger is a MODIFIED version. This does not create
a paid-functional-access exception for unmodified versions: section 5
still applies to both original and modified hosted software. If a copy
of an executable is supplied, section 4 independently requires immediate
source delivery to its recipient. A network offer alone does not replace
that executable-delivery obligation.

9. Contributions and third-party licenses

Contributors retain ownership of their contributions. A contribution
intended for integration must be accompanied by an explicit permission
from the person entitled to give it, allowing its incorporation,
reproduction, modification and redistribution under this identified
Community No-Resale License as supplied with the covered copy,
including the permissions needed for downstream recipients to exercise
it.
Submission or a Git author field alone is not presumed to be a transfer
of title or a sufficient grant. No general authorization to relicense
contributions under future, different terms is presumed.

Third-Party Material keeps its own licenses, copyrights and permissions.
Preserve the notices and satisfy the obligations that apply to the
parts actually redistributed. This CCS license neither removes those
permissions nor imposes its no-resale or source conditions on separately
licensed components as such. It cannot authorize copying of material
for which the necessary rights are absent.

10. Official identity and public modified forks

Unchanged CCS copies may retain the official CCS name, logo and identity
as they accompany those copies, without implying new endorsement or a
separate trademark license. A Public Modified Fork must use a distinct
name and logo as its own identity. It may accurately state its CCS
origin and must preserve the applicable copyright and license notices.
An origin statement is not permission to present a fork as official.

No general trademark license or independent right to use official
artwork as another product's identity is granted. The limited permission
to retain identity on unchanged copies and to identify origin does not
certify that any mark is registered, available or exclusively owned.
Third-party names and marks retain their own rights. Do not falsely
claim affiliation, endorsement or support by Shibxtshi, Valve, FACEIT,
OpenAI or other unaffiliated parties.

11. No additional contractual warranty; lawful liability limits

Covered Material is offered "AS IS", without an additional contractual
